UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 4, Lesson 2: Different perspectives on first contact', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Different perspectives on first contact come from European journals, later histories and First Nations oral histories and published accounts. Students present more than one viewpoint and mark what each source can and cannot show.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWhy is “Some people… while others…” a useful sentence frame for contact perspectives?\n\nFEEDBACK\nIt holds more than one viewpoint without pretending there was only one experience.\n\nWATCH FOR\nUsing only the explorer’s journal as the whole truth silences First Nations experiences.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nCompare two published sources on early contact (one European-origin, one First Nations-authored or Community-approved). Complete a perspectives table and a fair “Some… others…” paragraph. List sources.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"HSIE","learning_intention":"Different perspectives on first contact come from European journals, later histories and First Nations oral histories and published accounts. Students present more than one viewpoint and mark what each source can and cannot show.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Apply and respond","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: local map, photo or interview notes"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["HS2-HIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Compare two published sources on early contact (one European-origin, one First Nations-authored or Community-approved). Complete a perspectives table and a fair “Some… others…” paragraph. List sources. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: It holds more than one viewpoint without pretending there was only one experience. Reasoning: I used perspective by attending to source rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Using only the explorer’s journal as the whole truth silences First Nations experiences. Correction: It holds more than one viewpoint without pretending there was only one experience. Why: the correction restores accurate use of perspective.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Using only the explorer’s journal as the whole truth silences First Nations experiences. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating perspective on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with perspective, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"It holds more than one viewpoint without pretending there was only one experience. This matches the lesson skill for perspective.","options":["Using only the explorer’s journal as the whole truth silences First Nations experiences.","Ignore the source and invent a more exciting story.","Copy the model places and call that independent work.","It holds more than one viewpoint without pretending there was only one experience."],"question":"Why is “Some people… while others…” a useful sentence frame for contact perspectives?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Using only the explorer’s journal as the whole truth silences First Nations experiences. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Different perspectives on first contact come from European journals, later histories and First Nations oral histories and published accounts. Students present…","It holds more than one viewpoint without pretending there was only one experience.","Careful use of source with clear evidence.","Using only the explorer’s journal as the whole truth silences First Nations experiences."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising perspective?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named source, not by copying the model.","options":["Treat a missing fact as if it were recorded in a source.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the source used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right."],"question":"A learner is practising perspective. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"It holds more than one viewpoint without pretending there was only one experience. uses sources and places appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Copy the model places and call that independent work.","It holds more than one viewpoint without pretending there was only one experience.","Using only the explorer’s journal as the whole truth silences First Nations experiences.","Ignore the source and invent a more exciting story."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about perspective?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about perspective.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Using only the explorer’s journal as the whole truth silences First Nations experiences."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about source?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Treat a missing fact as if it were recorded in a source.","Ignore the source and invent a more exciting story.","Example: Source A (European journal) describes a brief meeting on a beach. Source B (a published First Nations historian or educator text)…","Using only the explorer’s journal as the whole truth silences First Nations experiences."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Different perspectives on first contact come from European journals, later histories and First Nations oral… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Compare two published sources on early contact (one European-origin, one First Nations-authored or Community-approved). Complete a perspectives…","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the model places and call that independent work.","Using only the explorer’s journal as the whole truth silences First Nations experiences."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of perspective?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating perspective on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: It holds more than one viewpoint without pretending there was only one experience. Reasoning: I used perspective by attending to source rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with perspective, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Using only the explorer’s journal as the whole truth silences First Nations experiences. Correction: It holds more than one viewpoint without pretending there was only one experience. Why: the correction restores accurate use of perspective.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-hsie-y4-w04-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA HSIE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (HS2-*); content-point mapping and local First Nations protocols remain pending human review.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise perspective.","Read the mission before you start mapping or using sources.","Keep the place, people or source you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the source or place detail that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore sources or respectful practice.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread names, places, dates and sequence.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact source detail or place feature before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key evidence.","Say what each source or place detail shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or use of sources, not only how neat the poster looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, person or source that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new sources or places."]},"subject":"History","success_criteria":["I can use perspective with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify source and explain why it matters.","I can practise viewpoint independently on a new prompt.","I can notice evidence needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Different perspectives on first contact come from European journals, later histories and First Nations oral histories and published accounts. Students present more than one viewpoint and mark what each source can and cannot show. Supported records follow the actual sources and…","options":["A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","Different perspectives on first contact come from European journals, later histories and First Nations oral histories and published accounts. Students present more than one viewpoint and mark what each source can and cannot show."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about perspective?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Source A (European journal) describes a brief meeting on a beach. Source B (a published First Nations historian or educator text) explains that ships arriving without permission were a serious intrusion on Country. Students write “According to A… According to B…”\n\nTRY FIRST\nWhy is “Some people… while others…” a useful sentence frame for contact perspectives?\n\nFEEDBACK\nIt holds more than one viewpoint without pretending there was only one experience.","explain":"Different perspectives on first contact come from European journals, later histories and First Nations oral histories and published accounts. Students present more than one viewpoint and mark what each source can and cannot show.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Using only the explorer’s journal as the whole truth silences First Nations experiences. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Perspectives on contact.","caption":"Some… others…","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Viewpoint A</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">European journal</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Viewpoint B</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">First Nations account</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Example: Source A (European journal) describes a brief meeting on a beach. Source B (a published First Nations historian Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Example: Source A (European journal) describes a brief meeting on a beach. Source B (a published First Nations historian Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task.","options":["A guess with no evidence.","Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about perspective?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Source A (European journal) describes a brief meeting on a beach. Source B (a published First Nations historian or educator text) explains that ships arriving without permission were a serious intrusion on Country. Students write “According to A… According to B…” Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show HSIE thinking. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Perspectives table.","caption":"Two published sources.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Fair paragraph</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">both named</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">journal = whole truth</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.","A claim unrelated to the source or place."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about perspective?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nUsing only the explorer’s journal as the whole truth silences First Nations experiences. Here is a more productive model: Example: Source A (European journal) describes a brief meeting on a beach. Source B (a published First Nations historian or educator text) explains that ships arriving without permission were a serious intrusion on Country. Students write “According to A… According to B…”\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nIt holds more than one viewpoint without pretending there was only one experience.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Using only the explorer’s journal as the whole truth silences First Nations experiences. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task.","options":["A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about perspective?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nDifferent perspectives on first contact come from European journals, later histories and First Nations oral histories and published accounts. Students present more than one viewpoint and mark what each source can and cannot show. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same HSIE idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Transfer to a new inquiry"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 4, Lesson 2: Different perspectives on first contact","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"First contacts and exploration","week":4,"week_in_unit":4,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: Source A (European journal) describes a brief meeting on a beach. Source B (a published First Nations historian or educator text) explains that ships arriving without permission were a serious intrusion on Country. Students write “According to A… According to B…”\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the sources, places or community details used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Perspectives on contact.","caption":"Some… others…","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-